⚡ The Big-O Cheat Sheet

Choosing the wrong collection can ruin your application's performance. Use this quick lookup table to select the right tool for the job.

Interface	Implementation	Access (Get)	Insertion	Deletion	Ordering / Features
List	ArrayList	🟢 O(1)	🟡 O(n)	🟡 O(n)	Insertion order, index-based access.
List	LinkedList	🟡 O(n)	🟢 O(1)	🟢 O(1)	Doubly-linked list, fast sequential inserts.
Set	HashSet	🟢 O(1)	🟢 O(1)	🟢 O(1)	No duplicates, chaotic order.
Set	LinkedHashSet	🟢 O(1)	🟢 O(1)	🟢 O(1)	No duplicates, maintains insertion order.
Set	TreeSet	🟡 \(O(\log n)\)	🟡 \(O(\log n)\)	🟡 \(O(\log n)\)	Sorted naturally or via Comparator.
Queue	PriorityQueue	🔴 N/A	🟡 \(O(\log n)\)	🟡 \(O(\log n)\)	Min-Heap / Max-Heap behavior.
Map	HashMap	🟢 O(1)	🟢 O(1)	🟢 O(1)	Key-value pairs, fast lookups.
Map	TreeMap	🟡 \(O(\log n)\)	🟡 \(O(\log n)\)	🟡 \(O(\log n)\)	Sorted by keys.


🛠️ Core Interfaces & Code Snippets


📋 List: Ordered Sequences

Use when you need duplicate elements and predictable positioning.
java
// Modern initialization (Java 9+)
List<String> wizards = new ArrayList<>(List.of("Harry", "Ron", "Hermione"));

// Common Operations
wizards.add("Neville");          // Append
wizards.get(2);                 // Fast index access -> "Hermione"
wizards.removeIf(w -> w.startsWith("R")); // Functional removal
Use code with caution.

🛡️ Set: Unique Elements

Use when duplicates are strictly forbidden.
java
Set<Integer> uniqueIds = new HashSet<>();
uniqueIds.add(101);
uniqueIds.add(102);
boolean added = uniqueIds.add(101); // Returns false (duplicate ignored)
Use code with caution.

🗝️ Map: Key-Value Pairs

(Note: Map does not inherit from Collection interface, but it's an integral part of the ecosystem).
java
Map<String, Double> productPrices = new HashMap<>();

// Safe updates using compute methods
productPrices.put("Cyberpunk 2077", 59.99);
productPrices.computeIfPresent("Cyberpunk 2077", (key, val) -> val - 10.00); 
Use code with caution.

🧵 Thread-Safety & Concurrency

Standard collections are not thread-safe. If multiple threads access them concurrently, you risk throwing a ConcurrentModificationException or corrupting data.
• Legacy (Avoid): Vector, Hashtable (Slow due to total object locking).
• Synchronized Wrappers: Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<>()).
• Modern Concurrent Collections (Recommended):
	• ConcurrentHashMap — Lock striping for high throughput.
	• CopyOnWriteArrayList — Thread-safe mutations via array cloning (best for read-heavy scenarios).

🌟 Pro-Tips & Best Practices

1. Pre-size your collections: Avoid expensive array resizing overhead by passing an initial capacity if you know the size beforehand: new ArrayList<>(10000).
2. Favor Interface Coding: Always declare variables using the interface type: List<String> list = new ArrayList<>(); instead of using the concrete implementation type.
3. Immutability by Default: Use List.of(), Set.of(), or Map.of() to construct read-only collections that enforce safety.


